[Event "Polerio Defence: the defence itself"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/NMtH8AT9/4H2funNl"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we learn the Polerio. The calm way to answer the scary fried liver attack. } 1. e4 { He grabs the middle. Meet him nose to nose: pawn to e5. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 (1... c5 { The Sicilian. A great fight, but a whole other world. } 2. Nf3 d6 { Another day. Today we walk straight at him. }) (1... e6 { The French. Solid, and also not today. } 2. d4 d5 { A slow squeeze. We want the open game. }) 2. Nf3 { He pokes your pawn. Defend it and develop: knight to c6. [%cal Yb8c6] } 2... Nc6 (2... Nf6 { The Petroff. Very solid, very safe, very much not our study. } 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 { Level and quiet. We want the sharp one. }) (2... d6 { The Philidor. It holds, but it shuts your own bishop in. } 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 { He owns the middle and you are squashed. }) 3. Bc4 { The bishop is already glaring at f7. Answer boldly: knight to f6. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yg8f6] } 3... Nf6 { The two knights defence. You are daring him to jump in. [%cal Gf6e4] } (3... Bc5 { The quiet Italian. Just as good, honestly. } 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 { A calm game, and no fried liver to defuse. }) (3... d6 { Too meek. Your dark bishop is boxed in behind it. } 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 { He takes the middle for free. }) (3... Be7 { Safe and a little sleepy. } 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 { He is comfortable, you are just waiting. }) 4. Ng5 { There it is. The knight lunges at f7. Do not panic: hit the middle with d5. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yd7d5] } 4... d5 (4... Na5 { The right idea, one whole move too soon. He grabs f7 with check first. } 5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. d4 d6 { Your king is stranded and a pawn is gone. }) (4... Bc5 { The Traxler! Wild and famous, and the machine does not believe it. } 5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. Bc4 Qe8 { You get the fun, he gets the better position. }) (4... Nxe4 { Grabbing a pawn while your f7 is on fire. } 5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. d4 h6 { A pawn down and your king may never castle. }) (4... Qe7 { She guards f7, but she also blocks your own bishop. } 5. Bxf7+ Kd8 6. Bb3 { Castling is gone and you are simply worse. }) 5. exd5 { Here is the big one. Everyone takes back and gets fried. You will not. [%cal Yc6a5,Ga5c4] } 5... Na5 { The Polerio. Forget the pawn, bite the bishop instead. [%cal Ga5c4] } (5... Nxd5 { This is the fried liver, and it starts right now. } 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. Qf3+ Ke6 8. Nc3 { The machine says you survive. Would you enjoy it? }) (5... Nd4 { The Fritz. Sharp, playable, and a whole book of its own. } 6. c3 b5 7. Bf1 Nxd5 { Messy fun. Ours is cleaner. }) (5... b5 { The Ulvestad. Also real, also a lot to remember. } 6. Bf1 Qxd5 7. Nc3 Qc5 { Playable, but he is a pawn up and safe. }) (5... Nb4 { It eyes c2, but the pawn just runs past. } 6. d6 Nbd5 7. dxc7 Qxc7 { He has untangled and you have nothing. }) (5... Ne7 { Too passive. Now f7 has no help at all. } 6. d4 h6 7. Nxf7 Kxf7 8. d6+ { The fork arrives with interest. }) 6. Bb5+ { Check. He saves the bishop and hits you at once. Block with the c pawn. [%cal Yc7c6] } 6... c6 (6... Bd7 { Blocking with the bishop is playable, just less testing. } 7. Qe2 Be7 8. Nf3 Nxd5 { Fine for you, but the pawn stays his. }) (6... Nd7 { The knight blocks, and now e6 is a hole. } 7. Ne6 fxe6 8. dxe6 { His pawn rips you open. }) (6... Nc6 { You undo your own good move. } 7. dxc6 b6 { Taking back runs into a fork on c6, so the knight is simply gone. }) (6... Ke7 { Never walk the king into that. } 7. O-O Qxd5 8. Nc3 { His pieces pour out at your naked king. }) 7. dxc6 { He takes. Which pawn takes back? The b pawn, opening a column for your rook. [%cal Yb7c6] } 7... bxc6 (7... Nxc6 { Playable, but the pawn was the better recapture. } 8. d3 Bc5 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Same pawns, and you gave him a free trade. }) (7... Bd7 { Careful, that pawn is still moving. } 8. cxd7+ Nxd7 9. Qg4 { He wins a piece and hits g7 as well. }) 8. Be2 { The bishop crawls back to e2. Now the key move of the whole line. Kick that knight! [%cal Yh7h6] } 8... h6 { Yes! Never let that knight sit on g5. Now it must go home. [%csl Rg5] } (8... Bd6 { Natural, but the knight on g5 is still glaring at f7. } 9. d3 Nd5 10. O-O O-O { Playable. Kicking first was better. }) (8... Be7 { Sensible, and one move too slow. } 9. d3 O-O 10. O-O Rb8 { He is fine and the knight still sits on g5. }) (8... Nd5 { Your knight goes hunting, but his on g5 goes nowhere. } 9. d3 Nf4 10. Bxf4 exf4 { You traded off your own best attacker. }) (8... Bf5 { A good square for the bishop, but not the moment. } 9. d3 h6 10. Nf3 Qc7 { You played h6 anyway, a tempo later. }) 9. Nf3 { Home he goes. Chase him again: push the e pawn and gain a tempo. [%cal Ye5e4] } 9... e4 { Lovely. The pawn kicks him a second time and grabs space. [%cal Ge4f3] } (9... Bd6 { Fine, but the pawn push was a free tempo. } 10. d3 O-O 11. O-O Bf5 { Comfortable for him now. }) (9... Qc7 { The queen is safe there, but slow. } 10. d3 Bf5 11. O-O Rd8 { He gets to untangle in peace. }) (9... Bc5 { Active, but it lets him settle. } 10. d3 O-O 11. O-O Re8 { The pawn push was the punch. }) 10. Ne5 { The knight lands on e5. Challenge him: bishop to d6. [%cal Yf8d6] } 10... Bd6 { Perfect. A pawn down, every piece out, and he is still untangling. [%cal Gd6e5] } (10... Bc5 { Just as good. The bishop eyes f2 instead. } 11. O-O Qd6 12. d4 Qxd4 { Equal and lively. Ours points at his knight. }) (10... Qd4 { Greedy. The queen goes hunting far too early. } 11. f4 Bc5 12. Rf1 { He gains time on her and builds. }) (10... Qc7 { Solid, and it still hits the knight. } 11. d4 exd3 12. Nxd3 { Also fine. The bishop does the job better. }) (10... Be6 { Sensible, but the bishop belongs on d6. } 11. d4 Bd6 12. Nc3 { You lost a tempo getting there. }) 0-1


