{ Same position, but this time he defends the bishop instead of checking. } 6. d3 { He props the bishop up with a pawn. What is the one move you always want here? } 6... h6 { Right. Before anything else, throw that knight out of g5. [%csl Rg5] } (6... Nxc4 { Trading is fine, but the knight on g5 is the real problem. } 7. dxc4 h6 8. Nf3 Bd6 { You played h6 anyway, a move later. }) (6... c6 { Chipping at the pawn, but g5 comes first. } 7. dxc6 Nxc4 8. dxc4 Qxd1+ { Queens come off and the fun goes with them. }) (6... a6 { A useful little move, but not the urgent one. } 7. Qe2 Bd6 8. Bd2 Nxc4 { He is organised now. Kick first, always. }) (6... Bc5 { Developing while a knight sits on g5. } 7. Nc3 O-O 8. O-O Nxc4 { He is comfortable, and h6 was free. }) 7. Nf3 { Out he goes. Now hit him again and take the space. } 7... e4 { Yes! The pawn kicks him once more. [%cal Ge4f3] } (7... Nxc4 { Fine, but the pawn push was the bigger idea. } 8. dxc4 Bd6 9. Be3 Qe7 { Level. You let him breathe. }) (7... a6 { Still waiting. The moment was right now. } 8. Nxe5 Bd6 9. d4 Nxc4 { He grabs your pawn and stays solid. }) (7... Bd6 { Sensible, but slow. } 8. Bb5+ c6 9. dxc6 Nxc6 { He wriggles free and you are only equal. }) (7... Bc5 { Active, but it lets him take on e5. } 8. Nxe5 O-O 9. O-O Nxd5 { A pawn down for not very much. }) 8. Nd4 { He hops into the middle. Now develop with punch. } 8... Bc5 { Perfect. The bishop hits his knight and squints at f2. [%cal Gc5d4,Gc5f2] } (8... c6 { Also completely fine. } 9. O-O Be7 10. Nc3 O-O { Level and playable. The bishop was livelier. }) (8... a6 { Solid, and equal. } 9. O-O b5 10. Bb3 c5 { Nothing wrong with it, just slower. }) (8... Bg4 { Tempting, but the check comes first. } 9. Bb5+ c6 10. dxc6 Bb4+ { Very messy, and he is not worse. }) 0-1


