{ You have kicked the knight. He refuses to go home the normal way. } 9. Nh3 { To h3, not f3. He keeps the knight, but look how sad it is out there. [%csl Rh3] } 9... Bc5 { Good. Grab the best diagonal while his knight rots on the rim. } (9... Nb7 { Also right. That knight on a5 needs a new home. } 10. O-O Bc5 11. Nc3 O-O { The same position, a different order. }) (9... Rb8 { Perfectly good. } 10. d3 Bc5 11. O-O O-O { Level and comfortable for you. }) (9... Bd6 { Fine too. } 10. d3 O-O 11. Nc3 Rb8 { Equal. On c5 the bishop bites a little harder. }) (9... Bxh3 { You can take it, but your bishop is worth more than that knight. } 10. gxh3 Bc5 11. O-O O-O { Equal. Keep the bishop and keep the threat. }) (9... g5 { Hunting the knight. Playable, but it airs out your own king. } 10. d3 Bg7 11. Ng1 O-O { He tucks the knight back on g1 and your king side has holes. }) 10. O-O { He castles. Now bring your worst piece back to life. Which one is it? } 10... Nb7 { Exactly. The knight on a5 was doing nothing at all. It heads for d6. [%cal Gb7d6] } (10... O-O { Safe and good. } 11. Nc3 Nb7 12. Kh1 Bxh3 { The same plan, and you castle first. Both are fine. }) (10... Bxh3 { Still a little early. } 11. gxh3 O-O 12. Bf3 Nc4 { Equal. The knight on a5 was the real problem. }) (10... Rb8 { Good square, but the knight is the sad one. } 11. d3 O-O 12. Nc3 Nb7 { You got there anyway. }) 11. Nc3 { He develops. Finish the job and get your king safe. } 11... O-O { Lovely. Now the plan is simple: bishop takes h3 and his cover is gone. [%cal Gc8h3] } (11... Bxh3 { Now it works too. } 12. gxh3 Nd6 13. d3 Nf5 { Equal, with the two bishops. Castling first is tidier. }) (11... Bb6 { Safe, but your king was still in the middle. } 12. Kh1 Nd6 13. d3 O-O { You castled anyway. }) (11... Nd6 { The knight arrives, and the king is still homeless. } 12. Na4 Bb6 13. Nxb6 Qxb6 { He trades off your good bishop. }) 0-1
